Let $\zeta = e^{2\pi i/221}$, a primitive $221$st root of unity. Since $221 = 13 \cdot 17$, we have
$$\varphi(221) = \varphi(13)\,\varphi(17) = 12 \cdot 16 = 192.$$

The polynomial $g(X)$ in the given factorization is the $221$st cyclotomic polynomial $\Phi_{221}(X)$, which is irreducible over $\mathbb{Q}$ of degree $\varphi(221) = 192$. Its irreducibility is the key fact: it tells us that $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta)$ has degree $192$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, and that the minimal polynomial of $\zeta$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ is exactly $\Phi_{221}(X)$.

**Structure of the Galois group.** The extension $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta)/\mathbb{Q}$ is Galois (it is the splitting field of $X^{221}-1$, which is separable over $\mathbb{Q}$). Every $\mathbb{Q}$-automorphism of $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta)$ is determined by where it sends $\zeta$, and the image must be another root of $\Phi_{221}(X)$ — i.e., another **primitive** $221$st root of unity. The primitive $221$st roots of unity are precisely $\zeta^a$ with $\gcd(a, 221) = 1$. Therefore
$$\operatorname{Gal}(\mathbb{Q}(\zeta)/\mathbb{Q}) = \{ \sigma_a : \zeta \mapsto \zeta^a \mid a \in (\mathbb{Z}/221\mathbb{Z})^\times \} \cong (\mathbb{Z}/221\mathbb{Z})^\times.$$

Since $|\!\operatorname{Gal}| = \varphi(221) = 192 = [\mathbb{Q}(\zeta):\mathbb{Q}]$, this accounts for the entire Galois group: an automorphism $\zeta \mapsto \zeta^a$ exists **if and only if** $\gcd(a, 221) = 1$.

